#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""adlab-admin — the ONLY root entry point for the Cockpit AD Lab plugin.

Same contract as wg-admin / hs-admin (see cockpit-wireguard/docs/CONTRACT.md):
  - the plugin never runs raw commands; it calls this helper with a narrow verb
  - every verb prints ONE JSON object on stdout and NOTHING else
  - diagnostics, warnings and errors go to stderr
  - exit 0 = success; on failure stdout carries {"error": ...} and exit != 0

Install:  /usr/local/sbin/adlab-admin, mode 0755, root:root
Call as:  cockpit.spawn(["/usr/local/sbin/adlab-admin", VERB, ...],
                        { superuser: "require", err: "message" })

Design rules, all load-bearing:
  - The verb table (VERBS) is the single source of truth for the UI: the
    `schema` verb dumps it as JSON and the plugin builds every control,
    form and modal from that dump. Change a verb here and the UI follows.
  - The lab is ROOTFUL podman; every domain operation is `podman exec`
    into a DC container (host samba-tool cannot authenticate over ldap://).
  - GPO and DNS writes target the PDC EMULATOR, discovered live via
    `fsmo show`, never hardcoded — SYSVOL replication is single-master
    with --delete-after, so writes anywhere else are silently deleted.
  - Passwords NEVER appear on argv or in the audit log. They travel on
    stdin (samba-tool's getpass falls back to stdin when there is no tty)
    or through a mode-600 authfile created and removed inside the
    container in one bash -c.
  - Every invocation is appended to /var/log/adlab-admin.log (verb + arg
    names, never values of password-typed args) — this is the plugin's
    "API activity" data plane.
  - Pure logic (parsers, validation, dispatch) is separated from effects
    (Runner) so the whole verb surface is unit-testable without podman.

stdlib only. No pip. Python 3.9+.
"""

